  [1mThe fork:[0m there is no [38;5;153muserId[39m anywhere in this codebase to pass. [38;5;153mindex.html:8-12[39m collects only username and password,
  and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub that fabricates its own response. So "add a userId parameter" can only mean one of a few
  different things, and they differ in what gets built afterward:

  - [1m[38;5;153mlogin()[39m returns the userId instead of taking one[0m — the server identifies the user; the caller can't know the ID
    before authenticating. Costs a change to the return shape ([38;5;153mapp.js:7[39m) and the caller, not the signature. Rules out
    nothing; this is the shape most auth flows converge on.
  - [1mClient-generated correlation ID passed in[0m — [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m at submit time, threaded through so a login attempt
    can be tied to later events. Buys you tracking of [3mattempts[0m (including failed ones). Costs an ID-generation decision
    and, to be useful, somewhere to persist it.
  - [1mCaller-supplied userId parameter, value wired later[0m — literally the signature change you asked for, with the call
    site passing a placeholder. Cheap now, but a parameter no caller can populate tends to rot.

  The first two are cheap to reverse in a stub this small. The third is cheap now and awkward later.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the return-value option, if "track who logged in" means recording the authenticated identity. A
  parameter can't carry that information at call time. But if you actually want to correlate a login [3mattempt[0m with other
  client events, the correlation-ID option is the right one and the parameter belongs in the signature.
